WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.980
Speaker 1: Here's just a wild, wild report from&nbsp;
Vanity Fair's Bess Levin. Donald Trump thought he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:15.360
could sue Congress for impeaching him. He thought&nbsp;
he could sue Congress because as legislators, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:21.300
impeached him. Now, what's not clear is whether he&nbsp;
thought he could sue Congress as an institution or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:27.780
that he could sue the individual members who&nbsp;
voted to impeach and later in the Senate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.640
convict. With Trump, it's never really clear what&nbsp;
he means. This entire article is worth reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:42.660
by Bess Levin, and the key part is actually from&nbsp;
Maggie Haberman, forthcoming book Confidence Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:50.520
which says When the House introduced articles&nbsp;
of impeachment against Trump for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:58.620
in 2019, Trump reacted with a familiar refrain.&nbsp;
According to the book, I'll just sue Congress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:06.540
They can't do this to me. Bess Levin adds It's not&nbsp;
clear whether Trump responded similarly after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.840
second impeachment articles, though, there's&nbsp;
probably a 50% chance that he did listen. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.700
would not really be a shock to anybody who's been&nbsp;
following the I guess we would call them trials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.920
and tribulations of Trump. He thought that pulling&nbsp;
in a special master after the Mar a Lago search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:29.040
warrant was going to be good for him. And it did&nbsp;
not work out that way. He looked directly into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.020
sun during that eclipse, even though he was told&nbsp;
not to. I mean, at the micro level and the macro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.640
level, he always believes things that aren't true&nbsp;
and believe certain things would be good for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.780
that actually are not. What's particularly funny&nbsp;
about Trump thinking he can sue Congress is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:51.240
right now we talked about this yesterday on the&nbsp;
bonus show. The argument that Trump's lawyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:58.860
are making to get a defamation suit against Trump&nbsp;
dismissed is that the defendant should actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:08.040
the government and not Trump. And so you have this&nbsp;
extraordinary situation where on and the point is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:15.060
you can't sue the government for defamation. So&nbsp;
it's defamation versus the impeachment. But on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:21.000
one hand, in court, Trump's lawyers are arguing&nbsp;
you can't go after the government for defamation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.580
And really, Eugene Carroll's gripe is with the&nbsp;
government and not with Trump, even though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.620
was Trump who said the things that are allegedly&nbsp;
defamatory. And meanwhile, after and during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.820
impeachment, Trump was saying, Well, I'll just&nbsp;
sue the government as if that's something you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:42.900
can do over a legislative duty that Congress has,&nbsp;
which is to consider whether a president has done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.520
something worthy of impeachment. It's all it's&nbsp;
all completely scrambled. You all know that. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:55.740
the other theme to this is the things that are&nbsp;
said publicly in the court of public opinion by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.680
Trump and even sometimes by his lawyers don't&nbsp;
translate to what they actually try to argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.540
in court. These are two different things. And&nbsp;
likewise, nobody's going to sue Congress for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.460
carrying out an impeachment. And of course,&nbsp;
it didn't happen. We will see what ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:17.280
comes of 2024. But Bess Levin sort of raising&nbsp;
the alarm here and saying, listen, the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:23.220
stuff was not the only wacky, dangerous, unhinged&nbsp;
stuff that Donald Trump was thinking at that time.

